import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Plan 0029 — the handl ↔ catopt boundary spike","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/plans/0029-handl-catopt-boundary-spike.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/plans/0029-handl-catopt-boundary-spike.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/plans/0029-handl-catopt-boundary-spike.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="plan-0029-—-the-handl-↔-catopt-boundary-spike" tabindex="-1">Plan 0029 — the handl ↔ catopt boundary spike <a class="header-anchor" href="#plan-0029-—-the-handl-↔-catopt-boundary-spike" aria-label="Permalink to “Plan 0029 — the handl ↔ catopt boundary spike”">​</a></h1><p>Depends on: <strong>ADR 0010</strong> (handl <em>is</em> the kernel — one language, many interpretations), ADR 0009 (the ladder; the calculus stage is gated on 0010), ADR 0008 (combinator presentation), and the existing bridge <code>catopt_discovery.handl</code>. External: <code>~/GitHub/handl-lang/host</code> (Peregrin) — <code>handl-language</code>, <code>handl-kit-arrow-lower</code>, <code>plugin-engine-{exec,typecheck,transform-rewrite}</code>, <code>handl-runtime/src/vm_primitives.rs</code> (host functions).</p><h2 id="motivation" tabindex="-1">Motivation <a class="header-anchor" href="#motivation" aria-label="Permalink to “Motivation”">​</a></h2><p>ADR 0010 decided that <strong>handl is the kernel</strong> rather than a Python reimplementation, on the strength of three facts: handl already is the arrow calculus, its language is data (ADR-0044), and it supports <strong>host functions</strong> — so catopt&#39;s tensor primitives can live outside the kernel. That is an <em>architectural</em> claim. It becomes a fact only when measured, and the three measurements are exactly ADR 0010&#39;s falsification #0.</p><p><strong>Spike, not implementation</strong>: the goal is to answer three questions and produce a go / split / no-go decision, not to build the integration.</p><h2 id="what-already-exists-don-t-rebuild" tabindex="-1">What already exists (don&#39;t rebuild) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-already-exists-don-t-rebuild" aria-label="Permalink to “What already exists (don&#39;t rebuild)”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>catopt_discovery.handl</code> — <code>store_to_handl(store)</code> emits handl fragments: op cells → <code>AstShapeDef</code> rows, law/object cells → <code>TransformDef</code> rows, handle cells → <code>!Native</code> records; every row carries a <code>catopt:</code> extension; <code>cell_from_handl</code> round-trips (provenance <code>imported</code>). <strong>The seam exists and was built deliberately.</strong></li><li><code>lab/bridge/combinator.py</code> — the monoidal/trace prototype (<code>BASIS</code>, <code>dag_to_comb</code>/<code>comb_to_dag</code>), which is what a <code>Term</code> encoding would replace.</li><li><code>catopt-native</code> — the precedent for a Rust peer: optional, excluded from the workspace, maturin-built.</li><li>handl&#39;s engines and its own suite (4,288 tests) — the kernel side is verified by handl, not by catopt.</li></ul><h2 id="the-spike-—-four-questions-cheapest-first" tabindex="-1">The spike — four questions, cheapest first <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-spike-—-four-questions-cheapest-first" aria-label="Permalink to “The spike — four questions, cheapest first”">​</a></h2><h3 id="q0-·-can-catopt-s-language-be-expressed-as-handl-data-hours-—-decisive" tabindex="-1">Q0 · Can catopt&#39;s <em>language</em> be expressed as handl data? (hours — decisive) <a class="header-anchor" href="#q0-·-can-catopt-s-language-be-expressed-as-handl-data-hours-—-decisive" aria-label="Permalink to “Q0 · Can catopt&#39;s language be expressed as handl data?  (hours — decisive)”">​</a></h3><p>Encode <strong>one</strong> catopt law as a handl <code>TransformDef</code> (YAML) and <strong>one</strong> catopt term shape as an <code>AstShapeDef</code>; load them into a <code>Language</code> via handl&#39;s <code>merge_fragments</code>; then run handl&#39;s <code>RewriteEngine</code> over a small term and see the law fire.</p><ul><li><strong>Pass</strong>: the fragment merges (no <code>validate_transforms</code> error), the rewrite fires, and the result is the term catopt&#39;s own <code>apply_rule</code> produces for the same input (compare structurally + numerically).</li><li><strong>Fail</strong>: the vocabulary cannot express it — <strong>name the missing piece</strong> (a shape role, a <code>when</code> condition, a value form). A named gap is a result; a vague &quot;it doesn&#39;t fit&quot; is not.</li><li>Cheapest candidate for a first law: a <em>structural</em> one (<code>assoc</code>/<code>comm</code>-class, no attrs), because it exercises shapes and matching without touching guards or values. Second: a law with a guard (<code>cond</code>) — that is where the data-only ratchet (ADR 0010 #2) gets its first evidence.</li></ul><h3 id="q1-·-can-a-catopt-tensor-value-cross-the-boundary-a-day" tabindex="-1">Q1 · Can a catopt <em>tensor value</em> cross the boundary? (a day) <a class="header-anchor" href="#q1-·-can-a-catopt-tensor-value-cross-the-boundary-a-day" aria-label="Permalink to “Q1 · Can a catopt tensor value cross the boundary?  (a day)”">​</a></h3><p>Register <strong>one</strong> catopt tensor op (e.g. <code>matmul</code> or a pointwise op) as a handl <strong>host function</strong>; evaluate a 3–5 node tensor term through handl&#39;s exec; verify numerically against catopt&#39;s own <code>eval_term</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>Pass</strong>: the value crosses intact — dtype, shape, device, and fp64-exact (or within the documented tolerance) agreement with catopt&#39;s evaluation. The host function must be able to <em>read and write</em> a tensor (not only scalars).</li><li><strong>Fail</strong>: tensors cannot cross without a lossy encoding (e.g. only scalars/JSON) — <strong>name the boundary</strong> and record whether the fallback (catopt owns the tensor exec interpretation) is required.</li><li>This is the question the ADR&#39;s &quot;host functions&quot; claim rests on, and it is <em>not</em> answerable by reading — the docs say host functions exist; whether they carry a tensor is the measurement.</li></ul><h3 id="q2-·-what-does-the-boundary-cost-a-day" tabindex="-1">Q2 · What does the boundary cost? (a day) <a class="header-anchor" href="#q2-·-what-does-the-boundary-cost-a-day" aria-label="Permalink to “Q2 · What does the boundary cost?  (a day)”">​</a></h3><p>Time the two paths on a realistic term (~100 ops, a shape catopt&#39;s bench uses): (a) catopt&#39;s existing executors (generic / batched), (b) the same term through handl exec with host-fn dispatch.</p><ul><li><strong>Pass criterion is <em>not</em> &quot;faster&quot;.</strong> The kernel serves <strong>search and rewriting</strong>; the delivered module runs the hot loop. So the criterion is: <strong>host-fn dispatch must not dominate rewrite throughput</strong> — measure rewrites/second through handl&#39;s engine on a small law set and compare with catopt&#39;s e-graph on the same set. Pass = same order of magnitude; the exec timing is <strong>informational</strong>.</li><li><strong>Fail</strong>: rewriting through the boundary is an order of magnitude slower than in-process — then the split in Q1&#39;s fallback applies, or the boundary needs a batched crossing.</li></ul><h3 id="q3-·-which-direction-is-the-integration-a-day" tabindex="-1">Q3 · Which direction is the integration? (a day) <a class="header-anchor" href="#q3-·-which-direction-is-the-integration-a-day" aria-label="Permalink to “Q3 · Which direction is the integration?  (a day)”">​</a></h3><p>Prototype the <em>shape</em> of the dependency, not the whole thing:</p><ul><li><strong>(A) catopt embeds handl</strong> — a PyO3 peer like <code>catopt-native</code> (<code>maturin</code>), catopt calls handl for term/rewrite/typecheck.</li><li><strong>(B) handl drives catopt</strong> — catopt&#39;s language is a handl language; tensor ops are host functions registered by a catopt plugin; catopt&#39;s discovery/RL become handl tools.</li><li><strong>Pass</strong>: one of them is buildable in catopt&#39;s CI story (wheel availability, no 8 GB cargo target in the default path) <strong>and</strong> has a named <strong>seam test</strong> (round-trip + semantics agreement — the new verification boundary handl&#39;s suite cannot cover).</li><li><strong>Fail</strong>: neither fits without weakening a catopt invariant (core zero-dependency, the coverage floors) — then record which invariant blocked it and what would have to change.</li></ul><h2 id="decision-the-spike-produces" tabindex="-1">Decision the spike produces <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision-the-spike-produces" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision the spike produces”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>outcome</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>go (A)</strong></td><td>handl as a Rust peer; catopt keeps language fragments + host fns + adapters</td></tr><tr><td><strong>go (B)</strong></td><td>catopt becomes an application of the platform</td></tr><tr><td><strong>split</strong></td><td>handl owns language + rewrite + typecheck; <strong>catopt owns the tensor exec interpretation</strong> (the ADR&#39;s fallback)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>no-go</strong></td><td>the boundary cannot carry tensor semantics faithfully — the kernel stays a catopt prototype, and 0009 stage 4 is not justified</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="order-and-budget" tabindex="-1">Order and budget <a class="header-anchor" href="#order-and-budget" aria-label="Permalink to “Order and budget”">​</a></h2><p>Q0 → Q1 → Q3 → Q2 (fail-fast: expression first, values second, integration shape third, cost last — because cost only matters if the first three pass). <strong>Bounded: ~3 days.</strong> Each question is a retro with its measured answer, committed as it lands.</p><h2 id="risks-measured-from-this-box-and-handl-s-own-guide" tabindex="-1">Risks (measured, from this box and handl&#39;s own guide) <a class="header-anchor" href="#risks-measured-from-this-box-and-handl-s-own-guide" aria-label="Permalink to “Risks (measured, from this box and handl&#39;s own guide)”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Disk/toolchain</strong>: handl&#39;s debug target is ~8.5 GB (<code>CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0</code> is the documented mitigation); this host was at 93% full today (32 GB free after cleanup). Budget disk before starting.</li><li><strong>Memory</strong>: handl&#39;s guide says <code>cargo nextest -j 8</code> OOMs an 11 GB box; <code>-j 2</code> peaks ~5 GB. The spike must respect that, and catopt&#39;s own suite must not run concurrently.</li><li><strong>Second toolchain in catopt&#39;s CI</strong> — a real cost that Q3 must price, not assume.</li><li><strong>Duplication temptation</strong>: if the spike drifts into &quot;reimplement a little of it in Python for convenience&quot;, that is the drift ADR 0010 forbids — record it and stop.</li></ul><h2 id="falsification-of-the-spike-itself" tabindex="-1">Falsification of the spike itself <a class="header-anchor" href="#falsification-of-the-spike-itself" aria-label="Permalink to “Falsification of the spike itself”">​</a></h2><p>If Q0 passes but Q1 fails, the <em>right</em> answer is the split (handl owns language/rewrite/typecheck), and that is a <strong>success</strong> for the spike: it converted an architectural claim into a measured boundary. The spike fails only if it produces no named gap and no decision.</p>`,28)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};